===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
{{Han compound|艸|alt1=艹|余|c1=s|c2=p|ls=psc}}.

{{lang|zh|茶}}, then slightly modified into a more conservative 茶, originates as a graphical modification of archaic {{m|och|荼||bitter plant}}, used for “tea” in classical sources. The modern version is unrelated to the bottom component of [[條]].

===Etymology===
As [[tea]] may have originated from [[Sichuan]], where the native {{w|Yi people}} speak {{w|Loloish languages}}, {{zh-ref|Sagart (1999)}} suggests that the Old Chinese item was possibly originally borrowed from {{bor|zh|tbq-lol-pro|*la¹||tea}}, from {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*s-la||leaf; tea}}. {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} traces its ultimate origin to {{cog|aav-pro|*slaʔ||leaf}} (in Sidwell's 2024 reconstruction).

Alternatively, {{zh-ref|Qiu (1988)}} suggests that it might be a semantic extension of {{m|och|荼||bitter plant}}.

